1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,622
This one is hands-on, and it only works if you actually do it.

2
00:00:03,622 --> 00:00:05,680
I'm going to show you a real AI output.

3
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:06,999
You're going to score it.

4
00:00:06,999 --> 00:00:11,379
Then I'll show you how other people scored the same thing, and we'll talk about why

5
00:00:11,379 --> 00:00:13,331
that result is a problem you now own.

6
00:00:13,331 --> 00:00:16,286
You need nothing but somewhere to write a single number.

7
00:00:16,836 --> 00:00:17,846
Here's the ticket.

8
00:00:17,846 --> 00:00:20,540
A customer was charged twice for an annual plan.

9
00:00:20,540 --> 00:00:23,009
They've already gone to their bank about it.

10
00:00:23,009 --> 00:00:27,610
There's a deadline, April the first, and a consequence if it's missed: the account

11
00:00:27,610 --> 00:00:28,172
overdraws.

12
00:00:28,172 --> 00:00:32,829
And they've offered a probable cause, which is that their account email and billing

13
00:00:32,829 --> 00:00:33,839
email don't match.

14
00:00:33,839 --> 00:00:35,242
Here's what the AI wrote.

15
00:00:35,242 --> 00:00:36,309
Read both properly.

16
00:00:36,309 --> 00:00:36,870
I'll wait.

17
00:00:36,870 --> 00:00:39,114
Now score that summary from one to five.

18
00:00:39,114 --> 00:00:40,124
One means useless.

19
00:00:40,124 --> 00:00:44,502
Five means you'd be happy for a support agent to act on it without opening the

20
00:00:44,502 --> 00:00:45,399
original ticket.

21
00:00:45,399 --> 00:00:46,297
Pause the video.

22
00:00:46,297 --> 00:00:47,364
Commit to a number.

23
00:00:47,364 --> 00:00:49,833
Say it out loud if you're somewhere you can.

24
00:00:50,383 --> 00:00:54,591
Here's what happened when five experienced people scored that exact summary.

25
00:00:54,591 --> 00:00:56,252
Two, three, three, four, five.

26
00:00:56,252 --> 00:00:56,806
Same text.

27
00:00:56,806 --> 00:00:57,581
Same question.

28
00:00:57,581 --> 00:00:59,962
A three-point spread on a five-point scale.

29
00:00:59,962 --> 00:01:03,008
And every one of those people could defend their score.

30
00:01:03,008 --> 00:01:07,659
The person who gave it a two noticed the April first deadline had vanished, and that

31
00:01:07,659 --> 00:01:10,483
deadline is the entire reason the ticket is urgent.

32
00:01:10,483 --> 00:01:14,857
The person who gave it a five was reading it as a triage summary, where "double

33
00:01:14,857 --> 00:01:17,515
charge, wants refund" is genuinely all you need.

34
00:01:17,515 --> 00:01:19,896
They weren't disagreeing about the summary.

35
00:01:19,896 --> 00:01:24,105
They were disagreeing about what the summary was *for*, and nobody had said.

36
00:01:24,655 --> 00:01:26,619
Now sit with the uncomfortable part.

37
00:01:26,619 --> 00:01:28,801
Your number is somewhere in that spread.

38
00:01:28,801 --> 00:01:33,001
Not because you judged badly, but because there was nothing to judge against.

39
00:01:33,001 --> 00:01:36,329
This is exactly what happens in your organisation every week.

40
00:01:36,329 --> 00:01:37,856
Someone demos an AI feature.

41
00:01:37,856 --> 00:01:38,565
People react.

42
00:01:38,565 --> 00:01:40,311
Some are impressed, some aren't.

43
00:01:40,311 --> 00:01:44,129
That reaction gets called a quality bar, and decisions get made on it.

44
00:01:44,129 --> 00:01:45,166
But it isn't a bar.

45
00:01:45,166 --> 00:01:49,748
It's five private opinions about five private definitions of good, and whichever one

46
00:01:49,748 --> 00:01:53,403
belongs to the most senior person in the room becomes the decision.

47
00:01:53,403 --> 00:01:56,567
Notice what this means for the idea from the last lecture.

48
00:01:56,567 --> 00:02:01,040
You can't measure a distribution with an instrument that gives a different reading

49
00:02:01,040 --> 00:02:02,677
depending on who's holding it.

50
00:02:02,677 --> 00:02:05,950
Before you can measure the model, you have to fix the ruler.

51
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:10,821
Now watch what happens when we stop asking "is it good" and start asking six

52
00:02:10,821 --> 00:02:11,902
specific questions.

53
00:02:11,902 --> 00:02:13,892
Is the core billing issue captured?

54
00:02:13,892 --> 00:02:14,119
Yes.

55
00:02:14,119 --> 00:02:15,768
Is the required action clear?

56
00:02:15,768 --> 00:02:15,995
Yes.

57
00:02:15,995 --> 00:02:17,360
Are deadlines preserved?

58
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:18,952
No, April the first is gone.

59
00:02:18,952 --> 00:02:20,601
Is the consequence preserved?

60
00:02:20,601 --> 00:02:22,079
No, the overdraft is gone.

61
00:02:22,079 --> 00:02:23,785
Is the diagnostic detail kept?

62
00:02:23,785 --> 00:02:27,936
No, the mismatched email, which is very likely the actual cause, is gone.

63
00:02:27,936 --> 00:02:29,244
Did it invent anything?

64
00:02:29,244 --> 00:02:30,438
No. Three out of six.

65
00:02:30,438 --> 00:02:35,043
And notice something important: the three it failed are the three that would have

66
00:02:35,043 --> 00:02:38,171
let an agent resolve this without reopening the ticket.

67
00:02:38,171 --> 00:02:39,251
Now score it again.

68
00:02:39,251 --> 00:02:44,027
Almost everyone lands in the same place, because they're answering questions instead

69
00:02:44,027 --> 00:02:45,107
of having feelings.

70
00:02:45,657 --> 00:02:49,238
Here's the before and after, and this picture is the whole lecture.

71
00:02:49,238 --> 00:02:50,948
The top row is where we started.

72
00:02:50,948 --> 00:02:54,475
Five reviewers, a spread of three points, and no way to settle it.

73
00:02:54,475 --> 00:02:58,857
The bottom row is the same five people, scoring the same summary, on the same day.

74
00:02:58,857 --> 00:03:03,132
The only thing that changed is that six questions got written down before anyone

75
00:03:03,132 --> 00:03:03,506
looked.

76
00:03:03,506 --> 00:03:05,964
The spread collapses from three points to one.

77
00:03:05,964 --> 00:03:10,239
That collapse is the entire trick, and I want to be precise about what it isn't.

78
00:03:10,239 --> 00:03:14,407
The rubric didn't make anybody smarter, and it didn't make the summary better.

79
00:03:14,407 --> 00:03:16,705
The summary is exactly as flawed as it was.

80
00:03:16,705 --> 00:03:21,087
What changed is that the disagreement moved out of people's heads and onto a page,

81
00:03:21,087 --> 00:03:22,477
where it can be discussed.

82
00:03:22,477 --> 00:03:25,843
And that's the difference between a quality bar and an opinion.

83
00:03:26,393 --> 00:03:30,251
Let's name what you just did, because it's more significant than it felt.

84
00:03:30,251 --> 00:03:34,532
You took an unanswerable question, is this good, and swapped it for six questions

85
00:03:34,532 --> 00:03:35,854
that each have an answer.

86
00:03:35,854 --> 00:03:38,338
The box on screen gives you the formal version.

87
00:03:38,338 --> 00:03:40,822
What I want you to notice is the word *before*.

88
00:03:40,822 --> 00:03:42,883
That word is doing almost all the work.

89
00:03:42,883 --> 00:03:47,270
Write your questions after you've seen the output and you will, without meaning to,

90
00:03:47,270 --> 00:03:49,120
write questions it happens to pass.

91
00:03:49,120 --> 00:03:50,229
Every team does this.

92
00:03:50,229 --> 00:03:54,510
It's why so many quality bars are quietly set by the thing they're supposed to be

93
00:03:54,510 --> 00:03:54,933
judging.

94
00:03:54,933 --> 00:03:59,214
On the number: fewer than four and you'll miss the failure that actually matters.

95
00:03:59,214 --> 00:04:03,548
More than eight and your reviewers stop reading carefully and start ticking boxes.

96
00:04:03,548 --> 00:04:06,191
Six is about the number people genuinely complete.

97
00:04:06,191 --> 00:04:08,939
And a rubric is the atom of everything that follows.

98
00:04:08,939 --> 00:04:12,586
Run it across fifty tickets instead of one and you have a golden set.

99
00:04:12,586 --> 00:04:16,867
Turn "three out of six" into a pass rate across those fifty and you have an eval.

100
00:04:16,867 --> 00:04:21,200
Put a threshold on that pass rate and you have an acceptance criterion you can put

101
00:04:21,200 --> 00:04:21,729
in a spec.

102
00:04:21,729 --> 00:04:25,799
That's Sections 3 and 4, and you've just done the first version of it in five

103
00:04:25,799 --> 00:04:26,221
minutes.

104
00:04:26,771 --> 00:04:31,310
One thing before you move on, and it's the five-minute version of what you just did.

105
00:04:31,310 --> 00:04:33,904
The rubric template is attached to this lecture.

106
00:04:33,904 --> 00:04:38,442
Download it now, while you're thinking about it, and open it against something real:

107
00:04:38,442 --> 00:04:42,819
a summary, a draft, a classification, a suggested reply, anything AI-generated at

108
00:04:42,819 --> 00:04:43,359
your work.

109
00:04:43,359 --> 00:04:45,358
Write six pass-fail questions for it.

110
00:04:45,358 --> 00:04:46,331
Not five, not ten.

111
00:04:46,331 --> 00:04:49,627
Six is enough to be useful and few enough that you'll finish.

112
00:04:49,627 --> 00:04:53,841
You'll find at least one thing you care about that the current output silently

113
00:04:53,841 --> 00:04:54,166
drops.

114
00:04:54,166 --> 00:04:58,488
That finding is worth more than the rest of this section, and you got it in five

115
00:04:58,488 --> 00:04:58,921
minutes.

116
00:04:58,921 --> 00:05:03,297
Next up, we go properly under the hood: just enough of how these models work that

117
00:05:03,297 --> 00:05:05,891
you can make good calls, and not one slide more.
